In summary, FewRel RE provides a quantitative match across models (near-identical slopes for Dimension~1 and a significant Dimension~2 effect). NER provides directional consistency: the count-driven decline direction is preserved and the U-shaped rebound replicates, though magnitudes vary with each model's precision-recall operating point. Together, these results indicate that the degradation pattern is directionally consistent across architectures.

\section{Few-Shot Prompting and Confusability}
\label{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}

To test whether in-context demonstrations mitigate the BCS-driven confusability effect, we evaluate 0-shot, 1-shot, and 5-shot prompting on FewRel 80-relation RE at count 8 using the same extreme-BCS batch design as \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}. Each condition uses 50 batches (25 low-BCS, mean $\approx 0.36$; 25 high-BCS, mean $\approx 0.80$), drawn from the pre-constructed batch pool of \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}. Few-shot examples are sampled from the FewRel training split for each target type.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrl}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Low-BCS} & \textbf{High-BCS} & \textbf{$\Delta$ (pp)} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
0-shot & 0.682 & 0.498 & +18.5 & {[}+14.3, +22.7{]} \\
1-shot & 0.754 & 0.554 & +20.0 & {[}+16.3, +23.5{]} \\
5-shot & 0.715 & 0.488 & +22.8 & {[}+18.9, +26.8{]} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{BCS gap under few-shot prompting (FewRel, count 8). Demonstrations improve absolute accuracy on low-BCS batches (+3.3--7.2\,pp over 0-shot) but provide no benefit on high-BCS batches ($-$1.0 to +5.6\,pp). The confusability gap widens from +18.5\,pp (0-shot) to +22.8\,pp (5-shot). All metrics are micro-averaged across batches.}
\label{tab:fewshot_bcs}
\end{table}

Demonstrations improve absolute accuracy primarily on low-BCS (easy) batches, where type boundaries are already distinct and examples reinforce correct mappings. On high-BCS batches, where types are semantically confusable, demonstrations provide negligible benefit: the model still substitutes similar types at comparable rates. The net effect is that the confusability gap widens under few-shot prompting (+4.3\,pp from 0-shot to 5-shot), with all three confidence intervals excluding zero. This indicates that BCS-driven type confusion is a structural property of the schema composition rather than a deficit addressable through prompt engineering.

The 0-shot gap (+18.5\,pp) is smaller than the extreme-batch gap reported in \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled} (+25.5\,pp) because the two experiments use different test splits and batch sampling seeds; direct magnitude comparison across experiments is not appropriate.

